# `pluss::subsp::stats::ts::lag`

The series shifted later in time: position `t` holds the observation from `periods` steps earlier.

## Signature

```cpp
pluss::Series ts::lag(const pluss::Series& x, std::int64_t periods = 1);
```

## What It Does

`lag` places `x[t − periods]` at position `t`, so the series appears delayed by `periods` steps. It is how a regressor is built from the past: the pair `(y, lag(y))` is a first-order autoregression's data. The first `periods` positions have no earlier observation and are `NaN`. A negative `periods` shifts the other way and equals [`lead`](lead.md) of the opposite sign.

## Parameters

`const pluss::Series& x`
: A one-dimensional series in time order. A series with more than one dimension is rejected.

`std::int64_t periods`
: The number of steps to delay (default 1). A negative value moves the series earlier instead.

## Returns

A `Series` of the same length as `x`; observations shifted out of range are dropped.

## Errors

- A series with more than one dimension is rejected.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

The series delayed by one and two steps.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <cmath>
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace ts = pluss::subsp::stats::ts;
using pluss::Series;

const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
void show(const char* label, const Series& s) {
  std::cout << std::left << std::setw(14) << label << std::right << std::fixed << std::setprecision(4);
  for (double v : s.host<double>()) std::cout << std::setw(9) << v;
  std::cout << "\n";
}

int main() {
  const Series x = pluss::make_series({10.0, 12.0, 11.0, 15.0, 14.0, 18.0, 17.0}, f64);
  show("x", x);
  show("lag(x)", ts::lag(x));
  show("lag(x, 2)", ts::lag(x, 2));
}
```

### Example 2

A negative shift moves the series earlier, like `lead`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <cmath>
#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace ts = pluss::subsp::stats::ts;
using pluss::Series;

const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
void show(const char* label, const Series& s) {
  std::cout << std::left << std::setw(14) << label << std::right << std::fixed << std::setprecision(4);
  for (double v : s.host<double>()) std::cout << std::setw(9) << v;
  std::cout << "\n";
}

int main() {
  const Series x = pluss::make_series({10.0, 12.0, 11.0, 15.0, 14.0, 18.0, 17.0}, f64);
  show("x", x);
  show("lag(x, -1)", ts::lag(x, -1));
  show("lead(x)", ts::lead(x));
}
```


